{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"pleasant-reliant-nandu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Why this epic exists\n\nOn 2026-09-04 the Gerrit host went fully offline for 41 minutes. Detection worked: ten alarms\nfired within seven minutes. **Explanation did not.** The cause had to be reconstructed afterwards\nfrom EBS I/O counters, and the direct evidence — kernel logs — was never captured and is now\npermanently gone.\n\nThat gap is not incidental. It follows from a single structural property:\n\n> **Every signal we have about the host is produced BY the host.**\n> When the host degrades, the observation degrades with it — and the failure that most needs\n> explaining is precisely the one that destroys its own evidence.\n\nThis epic makes observability survive the thing it observes.\n\n## What tonight established, concretely\n\n- **No off-box log destination exists.** 15 CloudWatch log groups in the account, ALL belonging to\n  unrelated projects. The Gerrit host's logs live only in on-box journald and die with it. The\n  2026-09-02 five-hour outage had the identical gap; nothing changed between incidents.\n- **EC2 console output is not a substitute.** During the incident it returned bytes timestamped\n  two days stale; after the recovery stop/start it returns EMPTY. Unusable as a diagnostic.\n- **Three metrics have been silently dead for 7+ days** — `disk_used_percent{mount=/var/gerrit}`,\n  `gerrit_healthy`, `reviewbot_healthy` — making their alarms unfalsifiable. And `list-metrics`\n  still shows the NAMES, so every superficial check reports the telemetry as present.\n- **The primary data volume could not be confirmed mounted** during recovery. The best available\n  answer was inference from EBS read counts (49,834 vs 49 on a known-raw volume). Correct, almost\n  certainly — but an inference, not a measurement.\n- **The cause was IOPS saturation, on a dimension nothing alarms on.** Root reads pinned flat at\n  ~2,500 IOPS for 30 minutes at only 47% disk FULL. Neither disk-space alarms nor the (absent)\n  memory alarm would have caught it.\n\n## The distinction this epic turns on\n\n**Detection and diagnosis are different capabilities, and we only built the first.**\n\n`herbal-garish-monkey` did real work: it converted dead-man silence into a page across 15 alarms,\nand that is what caught tonight's outage in seven minutes. Nothing here supersedes it. But \"the\nhost is gone\" is where the information ends. There is no path from an alarm to a cause, and with\nno off-box evidence, restoring service DESTROYS the only record — forcing every incident into\n\"restore quickly or diagnose, never both\". That tradeoff is an artefact of missing telemetry, not\na law of nature.\n\n## Success for this epic\n\nA future unexplained outage can be explained afterwards, without the host being alive, and without\nchoosing between recovery speed and root cause.\n\nExplicit non-goal: a general observability platform. The bar is \"an incident can be explained\",\nnot \"we have dashboards\". Retention costs money — note the account already carries 15 log groups\nwith `retentionInDays: None`, a pattern not to inherit.\n\n## Children\n\n- `fab-dismal-haddock` — no off-box logs; an outage produced no diagnosable evidence.\n- `enviable-beloved-buffalo` — three metrics silently dead 7+ days; alarms unfalsifiable; the\n  `list-metrics` trap that hides it.\n\n## Related, tracked elsewhere — do NOT duplicate\n\n- `witted-invisible-roan` owns the missing MEMORY alarm (`mem_available_percent` is\n  measurement-only). Memory exhaustion is the leading hypothesis for tonight's cascade.\n- `smudgeless-friendless-longspur` owns IOPS saturation detection and the finding that epic 6202\n  bounds disk SPACE, not IOPS.\n- `unamused-cloudy-junebug` owns the missing `/var/gerrit` mount assertion — which\n  `enviable-beloved-buffalo` explains cannot currently be implemented as intended, because the\n  metric it would read is dead.\n- `herbal-garish-monkey` owns `treat_missing_data` correctness, including the unenumerated\n  `missing` value on the two EC2 status-check alarms that stayed silent through tonight's outage.\n\nThe boundary is deliberate: those four are about ALARMING on conditions. This epic is about being\nable to EXPLAIN what happened, which is a different capability and fails in a different way.","id":"80c4-dcb6-bb87-4810","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Observability that survives the host: off-box evidence and live-publisher guarantees so an outage can be explained, not just detected"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788560457094096001,"uuid":"59edf054-d26e-4efe-9337-7f4d2f12ae52"}